---
import Base from '../../../../layouts/Base.astro';
import FederalCalculator from '../../../../components/FederalCalculator';
import '../../../../styles/calculator.css';
import { federalRates } from '../../../../calc/us/federal';
const format = (value: number) => new Intl.NumberFormat('en-US', { style: 'currency', currency: 'USD', maximumFractionDigits: 0 }).format(value);
const sources = [
  { title: 'IRS Rev. Proc. 2025-32 — 2026 brackets and standard deduction', url: federalRates.filing_statuses[0].source_url },
  { title: 'IRS Publication 505 — 2026 estimated-tax schedules', url: 'https://www.irs.gov/publications/p505' },
  { title: 'SSA 2026 fact sheet — employee Social Security and Medicare', url: federalRates.payroll.social_security.source_url },
  { title: 'IRS Additional Medicare Tax — annual liability thresholds', url: federalRates.payroll.additional_medicare.source_url },
];
---
<Base title="2026 Federal Income Tax Calculator | CalcsDesk" description="Estimate 2026 regular federal income tax on wages with the standard deduction. See Social Security, Medicare, each tax band and official sources." breadcrumb="Federal income tax calculator" ancestors={[{ label: 'United States', href: '/us/' }]} developmentMessage="Calculator beta · Source checked; human review pending" application={{ name: '2026 Federal Income Tax Calculator', description: 'Wage-only estimate of regular federal income tax before credits, plus employee Social Security and Medicare.' }}>
  <header class="calculator-intro"><p class="eyebrow">United States / Tax / 2026</p><h1>Federal income tax.<br /> <span class="accent">Every step, shown.</span></h1><p class="lead">Estimate regular federal income tax on wage income using the basic standard deduction, before credits. Social Security and Medicare appear separately; this is an annual tax estimate, not payroll withholding.</p><div class="verification-line"><span class="status-tag">Beta estimate</span><span>Last verified <time datetime="2026-11-02">2 November 2026</time> · official-source check; human review pending</span><a href="#rates-sources">View sources ↓</a></div></header>
  <FederalCalculator client:load />
  <noscript><div class="notice"><strong>JavaScript is needed for interactive results.</strong><p>The rate tables, assumptions and worked illustrations below remain readable. Enable JavaScript to enter your own wages.</p></div></noscript>
  <section class="calculator-explainer" aria-labelledby="how-title"><p class="eyebrow">Understand the number</p><h2 id="how-title">One income. Several separate calculations.</h2><div class="explanation-grid"><article><span class="micro">01 / Regular income tax</span><h3>Subtract the deduction, then apply the bands.</h3><p>The tool subtracts the basic standard deduction from the wage amount, with taxable income floored at zero. It then taxes each slice of taxable income at its own rate. Reaching a higher band does not apply that rate to all your wages.</p></article><article><span class="micro">02 / Employee payroll taxes</span><h3>Keep the wage taxes separate.</h3><p>Social Security is {federalRates.payroll.social_security.rate_bps / 100}% of each earner’s wages up to {format(federalRates.payroll.social_security.wage_base)}. Medicare is {federalRates.payroll.medicare.rate_bps / 100}% without a wage cap. These employee contributions are not removed by the income-tax standard deduction.</p></article><article><span class="micro">03 / Additional Medicare</span><h3>Use the annual filing-status threshold.</h3><p>The extra {federalRates.payroll.additional_medicare.rate_bps / 100}% applies above the threshold for your return. Employer withholding uses its own rule and can differ from final annual liability. A joint return uses both spouses’ wages together for this threshold, but each person keeps a separate Social Security wage cap.</p></article></div></section>
  <section class="calculator-examples" aria-labelledby="examples-title"><div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">CalcsDesk illustrations</p><h2 id="examples-title">Two examples using the official schedules.</h2></div><span class="section-caption">Illustrations, not IRS-authored examples</span></div><div class="example-grid"><article><span class="micro">Single / $75,000 annual wages</span><h3>$7,670 regular income tax.</h3><p>Subtract the $16,100 basic deduction to reach $58,900 of taxable income. The first $12,400 gives $1,240 tax; the next $38,000 gives $4,560; the final $8,500 gives $1,870. Add those pieces to get $7,670 before credits.</p><p>Employee Social Security and Medicare total $5,737.50 here. Together with regular federal income tax, modeled federal taxes are $13,407.50.</p></article><article><span class="micro">Joint / $90,000 + $60,000 annual wages</span><h3>$15,340 regular income tax.</h3><p>From $150,000 combined wages, subtract the $32,200 deduction to reach $117,800 taxable income. Tax the first $24,800 at 10%, the next $76,000 at 12%, and the remaining $17,000 at 22%: $2,480 + $9,120 + $3,740.</p><p>Employee Social Security and Medicare total $11,475 here. Modeled federal taxes are $26,815 before any credits or other taxes.</p></article></div><p class="source-footnote">Both illustrations are generated from the sourced rates and covered by automated tests. They assume identical income-tax and FICA wage bases. They do not replace the official-example and human-review launch checks.</p></section>
  <section id="rates-sources" class="rate-section" aria-labelledby="rates-title"><p class="eyebrow">Look behind the result</p><h2 id="rates-title">The 2026 rates used.</h2><p class="source-footnote">Source check: 2 November 2026. Human review is pending. The tables apply to the 2026 tax year only.</p><div class="table-scroll"><table class="rate-table"><caption>Basic standard deduction and Additional Medicare threshold</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Filing status</th><th scope="col">Basic deduction</th><th scope="col">Additional Medicare begins above</th></tr></thead><tbody>{federalRates.filing_statuses.map(status => <tr><th scope="row">{status.label}</th><td>{format(status.standard_deduction)}</td><td>{format(federalRates.payroll.additional_medicare.thresholds[status.id as keyof typeof federalRates.payroll.additional_medicare.thresholds])}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div><details class="full-rate-tables"><summary>View all federal income-tax bands</summary>{federalRates.filing_statuses.map(status => <div class="table-scroll"><table class="rate-table"><caption>{status.label} · 2026 taxable-income bands</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Taxable income above</th><th scope="col">Up to</th><th scope="col">Rate on this band</th></tr></thead><tbody>{status.brackets.map((band, index) => <tr><td>{format(index === 0 ? 0 : status.brackets[index - 1].up_to!)}</td><td>{band.up_to === null ? 'No upper limit' : format(band.up_to)}</td><td>{band.rate_bps / 100}%</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div>)}</details><div class="official-source-list"><h3>Official sources</h3><ul>{sources.map(source => <li><a class="text-link" href={source.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">{source.title} ↗<span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a></li>)}</ul></div></section>
  <section class="calculator-faq" aria-labelledby="faq-title"><h2 id="faq-title">Before you use this estimate.</h2><details><summary>Is this my paycheck or refund?</summary><p>No. It estimates regular annual federal income tax before credits plus modeled employee payroll taxes. It does not use W-4 withholding settings, amounts already withheld, state/local rules or benefits. Dividing an annual figure by 12 only produces a monthly average.</p></details><details><summary>What if my W-2 wage boxes differ?</summary><p>This version assumes one wage amount is taxable for income tax, Social Security and Medicare. Traditional retirement-plan contributions and other benefits can make those bases differ. If that applies, this simplified estimate is outside its supported case.</p></details><details><summary>Are credits, extra deductions or self-employment included?</summary><p>No. Credits, itemized deductions, age/blindness adjustments, dependent deduction limits, tips/overtime/senior/car-interest deductions, business income, qualified dividends, capital gains, foreign income exclusions, AMT and other special taxes are outside this version. Use the official IRS tools or a qualified adviser for a full estimate.</p></details><details><summary>How does the two-scenario comparison work?</summary><p>Enable the comparison below the main result. Scenario A uses the inputs above; scenario B has its own wage and filing-status inputs. Both use the same limited engine and display annual amounts side by side, with B minus A showing the difference. This does not determine eligibility for a filing status or recommend a tax choice. Scenario B is not saved or included in the estimate link; printing includes the comparison while it is open.</p></details><details><summary>Why is the page still in beta?</summary><p>The rate data has an official-source check and the engine has automated schedule and boundary tests. Human verification and the full release checklist are still pending. This searchable beta retains its limited scope. No professional reviewer has been claimed.</p></details><details><summary>What happens to my inputs?</summary><p>The calculation runs in your browser; the form has no submission endpoint. Wage amounts are not saved automatically. If you choose Copy estimate link, the amounts and filing status are included in its URL fragment. Share that link only with people you intend to see those figures.</p></details></section>
  <nav class="calculator-related" aria-label="Related pages"><a href="/us/">All United States tools ↗</a><a href="/sources/">Source directory ↗</a><a href="/methodology/">How we test ↗</a></nav>
</Base>
